<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<speak version="1.1" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis" xml:lang="en-GB">
<!-- The Watch Below, Chapter 59: Orders. Point of view: Maren. -->
<p><s>Chapter Fifty-nine.</s></p><break time="800ms"/>
<p><s>Orders.</s></p><break time="1800ms"/>
<!-- p1 -->
<p>The river was full of ice and running high, and Ottery wouldn’t take her over except at first light.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p2 -->
<p>He took her once, on the hundred-and-twenty-fifth, in the grey hour before dawn, when the night’s frost had slowed the melt upriver and the floes came down thinner, and slowed and spun, and you could pole a punt through the leads between them if you knew what you were doing. Ottery knew. He stood in the stern without a word and took them through, and grounded on the west shore, and sat in the punt in the reeds with the pole across his knees and waited.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p3 -->
<p>The pit was flooded.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p4 · awe -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">The brown water had come up through the old fill from below and filled it to the top of the stone. The lion stood in it to its knees. It was scraping. Under the water now. She could see the mud boil up round its forefeet where the claws went in.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p5 -->
<p>It looked up when she came over the bank.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p6 -->
<p>It let her come closer now. Since the collar. She came down the bank on her heels to the edge of the brown water and stood there, five paces off, and it watched her and went on scraping.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p7 -->
<p>Then it came up out of the water.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p8 · tender: the lion close -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">It came up the bank, streaming, and stood beside her. Close. She could feel the cold coming off its wet coat. She stood very still. It put its head down to the water at the edge of the pit, beside her, and drank.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p9 -->
<p>She put her hand on its foreleg.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p10 · tender -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">Above the great paw, where the leg went up thick as a tree into the chest. The coat was short there, and wet, and under it the muscle was hard and moving as it drank. The hum came up through her palm.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p11 -->
<p>It lifted its head and looked at her.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p12 · tender: quiet wonder -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">It didn’t move away. It let her hand stay. Then it went back down into the water, and turned to the stone, and she stood at the edge of the pit with her wet palm against her chest and watched it scrape until Ottery called.</prosody></p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p13 -->
<p>Rowan sent for her on the hundred-and-twenty-seventh day.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p14 · the boy -->
<p>She’d been at Greyhold since the evening before, drawing stores for the patrol: lamp oil, because nothing burned without it now, and bread, and a new pole for Ottery. The lamp-tender was in the stores yard too, filling oil-cans at the cask. He’d walked Greyhold’s walls every dusk for thirty years saying the words over each lamp, with his licence on a cord round his neck, and he still wore it. He filled the cans and didn’t look at anybody. She was loading the cart when the boy came. The Captain.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p15 -->
<p>She climbed the north tower stair behind him. Past the second floor. Up the last winding flight so tight she had to go sideways. She knocked.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p16 · Rowan -->
<p>“Come.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p17 -->
<p>Rowan was at her table this time, sitting, with her hands folded on the table in front of her and a paper under them. By the fire, in the chair where Maren had never seen anybody sit, Captain Thorne. Square and grey-haired, in her riding coat, with a ledger open on her knee. She looked up when Maren came in, and down again.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p18 · Rowan -->
<p>“Shut the door, Ashdown,” said Rowan.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p19 -->
<p>Maren shut it.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p20 · Rowan -->
<p>“Sit down.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p21 -->
<p>There was a stool in front of the table. She sat on it.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p22 -->
<p>Rowan looked at her across the table for a long moment, with her quiet face and her iron-grey hair cut short. Then she said, without any change in her voice at all:</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p23 · tension · Rowan -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">“I know what’s under the hill.”</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p24 -->
<p>Maren didn’t move.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p25 · tension: even, dry · Rowan -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">“I’m inner circle, but I’m not Watch,” said Rowan. “I’ve never been down. I’ve never held the lantern. But I’m Captain of Greyhold, and when the council gave me the school they sat me down in a room in Hallowmere and told me what I’d be captain of.” She looked at Maren. “So I know. And I know you know. I’ve known since the night of the lamps. Osric Hale is a bad liar, Ashdown, and he’s been looking at you across the yard for a month like a man who’s given away his last coat.” She paused. “And you’re a worse liar than he is.”</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p26 -->
<p>Maren sat on the stool and said nothing.</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p27 · Rowan -->
<p>“Captain Thorne went out to the island yesterday,” said Rowan.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p28 -->
<p>By the fire, Thorne turned a page of the ledger and didn’t look up.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p29 · Rowan -->
<p>“With Pell. In the morning, while you were on the road down here. At first light. Ottery took them, and wasn’t pleased about it.” Rowan’s hands didn’t move on the paper. “They went to the north end.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p30 -->
<p>Maren felt her face go hot.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p31 · Rowan -->
<p>“Captain Thorne saw the pit,” said Rowan. “And the water in it. And the beast standing in the water. And under the water, at the bottom, when it moved, she saw the stone. Dressed blocks. Grey.” She looked at Maren. “The same as the inner wall.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p32 -->
<p>Thorne closed the ledger.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p33 · Rowan -->
<p>“Your report said it digs,” said Rowan. “That’s all it said. Every report since the hundred-and-fourth day. <prosody rate="95%">It keeps to the north end, and digs.</prosody>” She tilted her head, very slightly. “It didn’t say where to.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p34 -->
<p>Maren looked at the table.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p35 · Rowan -->
<p>“Why?” said Rowan.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p36 -->
<p>She didn’t answer.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p37 · Rowan -->
<p>“I’m not angry,” said Rowan. “I want to know. You wrote fourteen pages about a roadblock on your first patrol. Every raider, every signal. Pell says you’d count the sheep in a field if he didn’t stop you. And you’ve sat on the bank of that pit for three weeks and watched that beast scrape at a course of the same stone as the inner wall, running under the river, toward us, and you wrote <prosody rate="95%">it digs</prosody>.” She waited. “Why?”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p38 -->
<p>Maren looked up at her.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p39 · Maren -->
<p>“Because it’s trying to get down,” she said. “To its own.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p40 · tension -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">The room went very quiet.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p41 -->
<p>Thorne, by the fire, turned her head and looked at Maren for the first time.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p42 · Rowan -->
<p>“To its own,” said Rowan.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p43 · urgent: unsteady, pouring out · Maren, unsteady -->
<p><prosody rate="105%">“What’s under the hill. It’s the same as the lion. Or it’s the lion’s… I don’t know the word. Its kin. Its kind.” Her voice wasn’t steady. “There’s a drawing in the black book. On one of the cut stubs. A forefoot. It’s the lion’s foot, Captain. I’ve put my hand in that print. And the lion came back to the island and lay down in the arch under the verse and wouldn’t leave, and it’s been digging down toward it all winter. Every night. When I put my hand on the door, something moved down there, and the lion roared. And something answered.” She stopped. “It’s not guarding the island. It never was. It’s trying to get to it.”</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p44 -->
<p>Rowan looked at her.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p45 · Rowan -->
<p>“And you didn’t want me to know that,” she said.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p46 · Maren -->
<p>“No.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p47 · Rowan -->
<p>“Because of what I’d do.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p48 · Maren -->
<p>“Yes.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p49 -->
<p>Rowan nodded. As if Maren had confirmed something. She looked down at the paper under her hands.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p50 · Rowan -->
<p>“Well,” she said. “You were right.”</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p51 -->
<p>She read it aloud.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p52 -->
<p>She didn’t hand it over. She held it flat on the table and read it in her quiet voice that carried to the back of the yard without being raised, and Maren sat on the stool and listened.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p53 -->
<p>It was from the council at Hallowmere. It had come the day before, by a rider with the crown’s own seal. It was short.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p54 · dread: read flat, official -->
<p><prosody rate="96%"><prosody rate="95%">The beast at the Old Ruin is to be secured before the faction calling itself the Restoration can reach it. The Captain of Greyhold is to take it alive by whatever means are judged surest, and bring it in chains to Greyhold, to be held under the old wall.</prosody></prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p55 -->
<p>Rowan looked up.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p56 · Rowan -->
<p>“A trap,” she said. “On the island’s west shore, where the reeds come down. Captain Thorne’s drawn it.” She nodded at the ledger on Thorne’s knee. “A pit-net. The crown’s beast-chain, the old chain, from the armoury. Not theirs. Ours. A carcass with a sleeping-draught in it, the strongest the infirmary can brew without words. Forty knights in the reeds, with the greyharts and Thorne’s boar.” She paused. “And you.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p57 · tension -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">Maren sat very still.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p58 · Rowan -->
<p>“It follows you,” said Rowan. “Everybody knows it follows you. Pell’s seen it. Ottery’s seen it. It followed you to the water every night in the autumn and watched you go. It let you take its collar off.” She said it without any weight on it at all. “You’ll go out at dusk the way you always do, and sit where you always sit. And when it comes to you, you’ll get up and walk down to the west shore. Slowly. The way you’d lead a horse. And it’ll follow you onto the net.”</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p59 -->
<p>Maren was quiet for a while.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p60 · Rowan -->
<p>“Why?” she said at last.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p61 · dread · Rowan -->
<p><prosody rate="96%">“Because if we don’t take it, they will.” Rowan’s voice didn’t change. “The Restoration. You’ve heard what’s on the road at the Ram’s Head. Pell’s seen the glow from the tower. Five thousand men, by the count that’s come to us. And a quarter-mile of beast lines.” She looked at Maren. “You’ve seen what they do to beasts, Ashdown. You saw it on the Redwatch landing. The hounds that came on with bolts in them. The ones with the eyes. You sat with Ide Darrow for three nights.” She waited, and when Maren didn’t answer, went on. “Think what that would be. A beast like that one. Ten feet at the shoulder and growing, with the ground moving for it. Bound. With some man’s hand on its neck and nothing in its eyes. At the head of five thousand men. At our wall.”</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p62 -->
<p>Maren thought of it. She couldn’t stop herself.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p63 · Rowan -->
<p>“If they take it,” said Rowan, “it’s a weapon. If we take it, it lives.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p64 · Maren -->
<p>“In chains,” said Maren.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p65 -->
<p>Rowan didn’t answer.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p66 · anger: voice she doesn't recognise · Maren -->
<p>“In chains,” said Maren again. “Under the old wall. You said so. You read it. <prosody rate="95%">To be held under the old wall.</prosody>” She heard her own voice and didn’t know it. “Like the other one. Down there. Next to it. So it can lie in the dark with chain on it for three hundred years and hear its own kin breathe through the rock. And we’ll light the lamps.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p67 · quiet -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">The fire ticked.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p68 -->
<p>By the fire, Thorne was looking at the floor.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p69 · Rowan, very quietly -->
<p><prosody volume="-5dB">“That’s not mine to decide,”</prosody> said Rowan, very quietly. <prosody volume="-5dB">“It’s not the hand that writes it.”</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p70 · Maren -->
<p>“It’s your hand that’s reading it.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p71 -->
<p>Rowan looked at her a long moment.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p72 · Rowan -->
<p>“Yes,” she said. “It is.”</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p73 -->
<p>She finished reading.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p74 -->
<p>She looked back down at the paper and read the last of it in the same quiet voice, without pausing, without any change at all, as if it were the same as everything before it.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p75 · dread: same voice, no change -->
<p><prosody rate="96%"><prosody rate="95%">And the farmer with the dragons, if he crosses, to be taken, or, failing that, killed.</prosody></prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p76 -->
<p>She put the paper down.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p77 · tension -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">Maren stared at her.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p78 · Rowan -->
<p>“That’s the council,” said Rowan. “Not me.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p79 · Maren -->
<p>“He put out the fires,” said Maren. “At Harrow’s End. In the autumn. When we sat on the bank. He put out the fires and got the children off the roof.” She turned on the stool. “Captain Thorne. You were there. You saw him.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p80 -->
<p>Thorne didn’t look up from the fire.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p81 · tension: very even · Rowan -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">“The council,” said Rowan, “has had a printed paper nailed to its own gate in Hallowmere with the farmer’s name on it as the sign of the one realm come again. It’s had another one since that calls him the one realm’s enemy. It doesn’t know which to believe, and it doesn’t care. It knows that there’s a man on the other bank with three dragons, who no book has ever heard of, and that every sign in the margin of the Watch’s black book is coming true at once, and he’s the last of them.” Her voice was very even. “It’s frightened of him. They all are. Hale told you that too, I expect.”</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p82 · Rowan -->
<p>“I’m frightened of him,” said Rowan. “I’m not ashamed of it.”</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p83 · Rowan -->
<p>“There’s one more thing,” said Rowan.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p84 -->
<p>She took something else from under the paper. A scrap of a different kind. A page torn out of a ledger, with writing on it in Pell’s square hand.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p85 · Rowan -->
<p>“Pell’s patrol took a man last night,” she said. “At the Redwatch landing. A sellsword. Off a Restoration boat that tried to come over in the dusk, to look at the island, we think. Two of them drowned. Pell pulled this one out.” She glanced at the page. “He talked. They do, when they’re wet enough. There’s a price on the Saltreach bank, he says. Paid by a man at the Ram’s Head. Two hundred silver for the farmer, alive.” She looked up. “And two hundred for you.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p86 -->
<p>Maren sat very still.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p87 · Rowan -->
<p>”<emphasis level="moderate">The knight with the lion,</emphasis>” said Rowan. “That’s what he called you. You and the farmer. Same price, he said. Same buyer.” She laid the page down. “They want you because the beast follows you. If they have you, they have it. That’s all you are to them, Ashdown. A halter.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p88 -->
<p>She folded her hands on the table.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p89 · Rowan -->
<p>“That’s what you are to us as well,” she said. “I’ll not pretend otherwise. We want you for the same reason. The only difference between us and them is that we’ll keep you. And we’ll keep it alive.”</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p90 · Maren -->
<p>“No,” said Maren.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p91 -->
<p>Rowan looked at her.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p92 · Maren -->
<p>“No,” said Maren again. “I won’t.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p93 · Rowan -->
<p>“Think about it.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p94 · Maren -->
<p>“I have.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p95 · Rowan -->
<p>“You’ve had a quarter of an hour.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p96 · anger: steady refusal · Maren, steady -->
<p>“I’ve had all winter.” She was surprised how steady her voice was. “I’ve sat with it every night since the freeze. I’ve sat in the bottom of that pit and watched it scrape at the stone over its own kin with its claws until they bled. I’ve put my hand on it. It let me. It put its head down in the mud and let me take the iron off its neck. I’m not going to walk it onto a net.” She looked at Rowan. “No.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p97 -->
<p>Rowan sat for a long moment.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p98 · Rowan -->
<p>“I’m ordering you, Ashdown,” she said. “As your Captain. I’m ordering you to do it.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p99 · Maren -->
<p>“No.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p100 · Rowan -->
<p>“You swore an oath.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p101 · Maren -->
<p>“I swore to keep the watch below,” said Maren. “I can’t say the rest of it any more. I’ve tried. It won’t come out. And I never swore to put another one down there.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p102 -->
<p>Thorne had put her hand over her eyes.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p103 · tension -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">Rowan looked at her across the table. Her quiet face didn’t change. It never changed. But her hands, folded on the table, on the council’s paper, had gone white at the knuckles.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p104 · Rowan -->
<p>“A third time,” said Rowan. “So it’s said. I’m ordering you to bring the beast at the Old Ruin onto the net on the west shore of the island at dusk, on the day I name.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p105 · Maren -->
<p>“No,” said Maren.</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p106 -->
<p>Rowan sat back in her chair.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p107 -->
<p>She looked out of the window for a while. At the river, high and brown and full of ice. At the island in the middle of it, dark, with the bare oaks.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p108 · tension · Rowan -->
<p><prosody rate="97%">“Then I’ll do it without you,” she said. “And it’ll go worse. You know it will. Forty knights in the reeds and no one it trusts to bring it in. It’ll fight. Some of my knights will die. It may die. The net may not hold it, and the chain may not, and then I’ll have to do it another way, and I don’t know what that way is yet.” She didn’t look round. “You know all that.”</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p109 · Maren -->
<p>“Yes.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p110 · Rowan -->
<p>“And you still say no.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p111 · Maren -->
<p>“Then it’ll go worse,” said Maren.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p112 -->
<p>Rowan turned and looked at her.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p113 · Hale (remembered) -->
<p>For a moment, just a moment, something moved in the quiet face, something like what Hale’s face had done on the archive step, under the carved line, when he said <prosody volume="-3dB">you don’t tell people things.</prosody> Then it was gone.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p114 · anger: cold, controlled · Rowan -->
<p>“You’re off Pell’s patrol,” said Rowan. “From now. You’re confined to Greyhold. The midden. The barn. The refectory. The dormitory. Nowhere else. You’ll not go to Redwatch. You’ll not go on the river. You’ll not go near the island.” She picked up her pen. “If you go over the wall behind the midden again, the way you did on the hundred-and-twenty-third night, the gate will be told to stop you, and they’ll stop you however they have to.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p115 · Rowan (remembered) -->
<p>Maren felt it go through her. <prosody volume="-3dB">The hundred-and-twenty-third night.</prosody> The hedge-meeting.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p116 -->
<p>Rowan didn’t look up from her pen.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p117 · Rowan -->
<p>“Captain Thorne will see you down,” she said.</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p118 -->
<p>Thorne walked her down the stair.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p119 -->
<p>She went first, with a lantern, though it was daylight; the tower stair had no windows. Down the tight winding flight, sideways. Past the second floor. Down. Thorne didn’t speak. Her boots rang on the steps.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p120 -->
<p>At the bottom, at the door into the yard, she stopped.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p121 -->
<p>She stood with her hand on the latch and didn’t open it. She looked at the door, not at Maren.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p122 · Thorne, very low -->
<p><prosody volume="-5dB">“I lifted my hand to him, you know,”</prosody> she said. Very low. <prosody volume="-5dB">“The farmer. In the autumn. The morning after the burning, at Harrow’s End.”</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p123 -->
<p>Maren looked at her.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p124 · grief: low confession · Thorne, low -->
<p><prosody rate="94%"><prosody volume="-5dB">“We sat on the bank all night,”</prosody> said Thorne. <prosody volume="-5dB">“Twenty of us. With the lanterns. Watching it burn. Because the orders said no knight crosses the river into the Marches, not for anything, not for any reason. And I sat on my horse in the middle of the line and watched a whole village burn, and heard the children.”</prosody> She was quiet a moment. <prosody volume="-5dB">“And then he came. Out of the dark. On the old general. And put the fires out. And in the morning when it was light he stood on the bank on the far side with the three of them behind him, and looked across the water at us, sitting there.”</prosody> She took a breath. <prosody volume="-5dB">“And I lifted my hand to him. I don’t know why. I couldn’t do anything else.”</prosody> She turned her head and looked at Maren at last. <prosody volume="-5dB">“It was the only right thing I did that whole night.”</prosody></prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p125 -->
<p>She opened the door. The thin spring light came in off the yard, and the noise of the hammering on the scaffolds on the river wall.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p126 · Thorne -->
<p>“Go to your barn, Ashdown,” said Thorne.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p127 -->
<p>Maren went.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p128 -->
<p>She crossed the yard under the scaffolds, with men on the planks above her lashing up the new timber along the wall, and the great arm of a throwing-engine swinging slowly round on the river tower as they set it, turning to face the Saltreach bank. She went into the Long Barn. It was dim and warm and smelled of the beasts.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p129 · quiet -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">At the end of the barn, in the biggest stall, behind the double doors with the iron bands, Thorne’s boar lay in the straw with its small red eyes open, facing the north wall.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p130 -->
<p>She picked up a fork.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p131 · Rowan (remembered) -->
<p><prosody volume="-3dB">And the farmer with the dragons, if he crosses.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p132 · reflective -->
<p><prosody rate="96%">She thought of narrow boot-prints and hobnailed ones, in the snow by her fire, one over the other. Knees in the snow. A scale the size of a round shield, warm, in the chest under her cot. Three dragons answering a roar across the ice from the hills above Harrow’s End.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p133 · Rowan (remembered) -->
<p><prosody volume="-3dB">You and the farmer. Same price. Same buyer.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p134 · quiet -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">She began to muck out.</prosody></p>
<break time="1800ms"/>
</speak>
